course: dictionary
lesson: 13496
title: Seeming but not real
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Illusory is an adjective. It describes something that seems real but is not. «The spinning stripes looked as if they were moving, but the movement was illusory.» The stripes were still. Only the movement seemed real.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. The word but tells you that how something looks is different from what is true. Try your meaning in the sentence: "the movement was not real, though it seemed real" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In formal writing, illusory describes a hope, an advantage or a feeling that looks possible or true but is actually false. «Her sense of safety behind the thin fence was illusory.» The fence looked safe, but it was not.
fact: Illusory means not real, though it seems real.
fact: Something illusory is based on an illusion, a trick of the eyes or mind.
fact: In science, optical illusions show illusory movement, size or colour.
fact: An «illusory hope» is a hope that looks possible but is false.
fact: The phrase «prove illusory» means turn out to be false.
exercise: «The moving stripes looked as if they spun, but the movement was illusory.» What does illusory mean? | Bright and shiny | Very fast and powerful | *Not real, though it seems real | Made of wood
exercise: In a science lesson, which example shows illusory movement? | *A still picture that seems to wobble when you stare at it | A cup filled with water | A car parked in a garage | A ball rolling down a hill
exercise: «The promise of a quick win proved illusory.» What does proved illusory mean here? | Was forgotten quickly | *Turned out to be false | Turned out to be true and certain | Was written down carefully
test: «Her sense of safety behind the thin fence was illusory.» Which phrase means nearly the same as illusory here? | Completely safe and strong | Very cold and wet | *Seeming safe but not really safe | Loud and noisy
test: In formal writing, which describes an illusory hope? | *Looks possible but is actually false | Is certain to come true | Is written in gold ink | Is shared by everyone in the class
exam: «The lake looked deep, but the depth was illusory: it was only a painted picture on the wall.» What does illusory mean? | Cold and wet to touch | Very deep and dark | Painted in bright colours | *Not real, though it seems real
